body=f'''<!doctype html><html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Parafoil model identification — actuator audit and validation</title><style>body{{max-width:1100px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 24px;font:17px/1.55 system-ui;color:#183044}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}}th,td{{padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd5dd;text-align:left}}img{{width:100%}}code{{background:#eef2f5}}h1,h2{{line-height:1.2}}</style><h1>Parafoil model identification: actuator audit and validation</h1><p>Research snapshot · October 9, 2026. No production simulator coefficients or flight parameters changed.</p>
<h2>Decision</h2><p>The brake mapping is corrected, but neither the old nor the newly fitted rigid model reproduces the held-out Hood River high-bank maneuver. The new fit improves Trout Lake bank response while leaving significant heading errors and worsening some vertical predictions. These coefficients are diagnostic candidates, not a validated replacement or a basis for controller optimization.</p>
<h2>Every available onboard log was audited</h2><table><tr><th>Log</th><th>Left released → full PWM, µs</th><th>Right released → full PWM, µs</th><th>Estimated left / right pull, mm</th></tr>{rows}</table><p>All six logs: channel 1 throttle, channel 3 left brake (function 190), channel 4 right brake (191). Throttle 1000–2000 µs; left normal, right reversed; trims equal released endpoints. No in-log changes to these mappings found in PARM records. This audit covers the six extracted onboard logs, not every telemetry-only session.</p>
<p>Line pull = signed PWM displacement × π × 55 mm / 360 × 0.1 degrees/µs. The 55 mm drum and 90 mm physical travel are user measurements; the nominal angular slope is from the <a href="https://hobbyking.com/en_us/corona-ds339hv-digital-metal-gear-servo-5-1kg-0-13-sec-32g.html">DS339HV product specification</a>. Effective winding diameter, no endpoint saturation, and released endpoint as zero pull are assumed. The user confirmed the left/right asymmetry is real. Absolute slack and canopy deformation remain unmeasured.</p>
<h2>Replay and fitting</h2><p>Native force, torque and inertia functions extracted from published revision <code>b2f6da3a183a68d3de6f2c721ec90fca0eab53d5</code>. Model state uses body-to-NED Euler conventions and all three gyro axes. Configured brake displacement is expressed relative to a shared 72 mm reference, retaining the legacy 45-degree coefficient convention; this is not a claim that 72 mm creates 45 degrees of physical canopy deflection.</p><p>Mass fixed at 1.385 kg for HR and 1.758 kg for TL, interpreting the user's without/with-lead configurations. Added mass is 0.373 kg. Default inertia remains unchanged because ballast position relative to CG is not measured. This limits the physical interpretation of fitted derivatives.</p><p>Separate flight fits use manual-flight samples below 25 degrees bank. Exclusion windows cover every validation maneuver plus a 10-second lead and 13-second tail around each start. Training uses angular acceleration, robust least squares and Gaussian-filtered body rates (0.2-second sigma, offline noncausal). Coefficients fitted: yaw damping, brake yaw authority, roll restoring derivative, roll-rate damping derivative and brake roll authority. A separate fixed roll damper remains in the source model.</p><p>Replays run three seconds from measured initial conditions with recorded commands and no attitude feedback. Zero-order-held brake PWM; original interpolated throttle input retained (1000–2000 mapping identical in every log). Midpoint integration at 0.01 s, checked against 0.005 s. Approximate constant wind: HR N/E 1.04/0.59 m/s; TL 0.32/−0.13 m/s. Wind is inferred, not pitot-validated.</p><img src="physical-mapping-validation.png" alt="Held-out replay comparisons"><table><tr><th>Flight</th><th>Start, boot s</th><th>Case</th><th>Bank RMSE, deg</th><th>Final heading error, deg</th><th>Vertical speed RMSE, m/s</th></tr>{replays}</table>
<h2>What the diagnostics establish</h2><ul><li>Halving the integration step changes final heading by less than 0.04 degrees in the checked fitted cases: numerical step size does not explain the large error.</li><li>Changing either wind component by ±0.5 m/s does not resolve the HR turn mismatch. This is a local sensitivity check, not a complete wind uncertainty bound.</li><li>Filtered gyro kinematics reproduce the key turns' integrated heading changes within roughly 3.4 degrees over three seconds. Raw instantaneous gyro-versus-attitude differences are much larger; vibration must not be fitted as aerodynamic acceleration.</li><li>Providing measured pitch and pitch rate improves TL bank errors from about 7.0/5.0/10.0 degrees to 3.7/2.5/5.6 degrees for the three cases. HR high-bank entry remains about 21.5 degrees versus 22.5 free. These are conditional diagnostics, not autonomous model predictions.</li><li>The existing articulated implementation has a pitch joint only; it cannot represent separate canopy/payload roll. Full articulated replay has not yet been run.</li><li>Several damping derivatives hit their zero bounds, and the fitted brake-roll derivative changes sign between flights. That is evidence against accepting this parameterization as an identified physical model, not evidence that the real canopy has no damping.</li></ul>
<h2>Next model work</h2><p>Separate payload attitude from canopy orientation and fit the translational force balance jointly with turn dynamics. Before introducing more degrees of freedom, obtain ballast position and canopy/payload geometry to constrain CG and inertia; evaluate the existing pitch-joint implementation with documented initial-state uncertainty. Preserve HR's high-bank turn as a validation challenge. Test left/right ordinary turns and multi-circle altitude retention before evaluating autonomous minimum-radius claims. The current data demonstrate a tight finite maneuver, not a sustained equilibrium.</p>
<h2>Reproducibility</h2><p>Scripts in the parent directory, run with WSL Python: <code>audit_servo_mapping.py</code>, <code>build_lateral_evaluator.py</code>, <code>fit_physical_mapping.py</code>, <code>fit_filtered_mapping.py</code>, <code>check_kinematics.py</code>, <code>diagnose_coupling.py</code>, <code>report_physical_mapping.py</code>. Evaluator build requires the previously extracted <code>tunable_evaluator.cpp</code>. Source hashes and revision are in <code>model-provenance.json</code>. Inputs and validation trajectories remain alongside this report. Earlier fit JSONs remain for comparison; <code>filtered-physical-mapping-fit.json</code> is the current corrected result.</p></html>'''
(O/'actuator-audit-and-model-validation.html').write_text(body)
